---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Experiment 11.2 - Making and Using an Electroscope">
    <div class="container">
        <h1>Experiment 11.2: Making and Using an Electroscope</h1>
        <p class="experiment-description">Gain practice in building a device that detects the presence of electrical charge and observe the basic methods used to charge an object.</p>

        <!-- Student Information Section -->
        <div class="student-info">
            <h2>Student Information</h2>
            <div class="info-grid">
                <div>
                    <label for="student-name">Name:</label>
                    <input type="text" id="student-name" placeholder="Enter your full name">
                </div>
                <div>
                    <label for="student-email">Email:</label>
                    <input type="email" id="student-email" placeholder="your.email@example.com" value="chuck@c00lnerd.com">
                </div>
                <div>
                    <label for="experiment-date">Date:</label>
                    <input type="date" id="experiment-date">
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Purpose Section -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Purpose</h2>
            <p>To gain practice in building a device that detects the presence of electrical charge and to observe the basic methods used to charge an object.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Materials Section -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Materials</h2>
            <ul class="materials-list">
                <li>Tape</li>
                <li>A clear glass</li>
                <li>A plastic lid to fit over the glass (The lid can be larger than the mouth of the glass, but it cannot be smaller. The top of a margarine tub or something similar works quite well)</li>
                <li>A paperclip</li>
                <li>Two 5 cm x 15 cm strips of aluminum foil (the thinner the better—do not fold the foil, rather cut it to this size)</li>
                <li>A balloon</li>
                <li>A pair of pliers</li>
                <li>Eye protection such as goggles or safety glasses</li>
                <li>Van de Graaff generator (optional but recommended)</li>
                <li>Small metal objects (paper clips, aluminum foil pieces)</li>
                <li>Plastic rod or ruler</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Safety Section -->
        <div class="safety-section">
            <h2>⚠️ Safety Instructions</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Wear eye protection at all times during this experiment</li>
                <li>Be careful when using pliers to bend the paperclip</li>
                <li>Handle aluminum foil strips gently to avoid tearing</li>
                <li>Never touch electrical outlets or wires during this experiment</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Background Information -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Background Information</h2>
            <div class="background-info">
                <h3>What is an Electroscope?</h3>
                <p>An electroscope is a device that detects the presence of electrical charge. When charged objects are brought near an electroscope, the foil strips inside will move, indicating the presence of electrical charge.</p>
                
                <h3>Three Methods of Charging Objects</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Charging by Friction:</strong> Charging an object by rubbing two items together where one transfers electrons to the other</li>
                    <li><strong>Charging by Conduction:</strong> Charging an object by allowing it to come into contact with an object that already has an electrical charge</li>
                    <li><strong>Charging by Induction:</strong> Charging an object without direct contact between the object and a charge</li>
                </ol>
                
                <h3>How the Electroscope Works</h3>
                <p>When a charged object is brought near the electroscope, it causes the electrons in the foil strips to move. If the strips develop the same charge, they repel each other and spread apart. This movement indicates the presence of electrical charge.</p>
                
                <h3>Van de Graaff Generator</h3>
                <p>A Van de Graaff generator is a device that creates static electricity at a much higher voltage than simple friction methods. It uses a moving belt to accumulate electric charge on a hollow metal sphere. The generator can create charges strong enough to produce visible sparks and can cause hair to stand on end when a person touches it. It's an excellent tool for demonstrating the principles of electrostatics and for testing the sensitivity of an electroscope.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Procedure Section -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Procedure</h2>
            <div class="procedure-steps">
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 1: Prepare the Paperclip</h3>
                    <p>Using your hands and the pliers, straighten out and then bend the paperclip so that it ends up looking something like what is shown in Figure 11.11A.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 2: Create the Loop</h3>
                    <p>Cut a thin slot in the plastic lid. Slide the loop of the twisted paper clip into the slot, then twist it 90 degrees so that the slot holds the loop in place. The loop should stand perpendicular to the lid. You may need to use some tape to hold it in this position (see Figure 11.11B).</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 3: Attach Foil Strips</h3>
                    <p>Poke a hole near one end of each strip of foil and hang the foil strips on the tiny hooks that are at the bottom of the twisted paper clip.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 4: Assemble the Electroscope</h3>
                    <p>Place the lid on top of the glass, so that the foil strips hang on the inside of the glass. You have just made an electroscope (in fact, truly sloppy). It should look something like what you see in Figure 11.11B.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 5: Test the Electroscope</h3>
                    <p>What does an electroscope do? An electroscope detects the presence of electrical charge. To see this, inflate the balloon and tie it off so that it stays inflated.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 6: Charge the Balloon</h3>
                    <p>Rub the balloon in your hair to charge it.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 7: Test Detection (No Contact)</h3>
                    <p>Slowly bring the balloon close to the loop of the twisted paper clip without actually touching it. The foil strips should start to move. If they do not, your balloon is probably not charged well. Rub it more vigorously in your hair or rub it in someone else's hair.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 8: Observe Movement</h3>
                    <p>Note how the foil strips move as you bring the balloon closer to the loop. Don't actually touch the loop with the balloon! Record your observations.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 9: Test Multiple Times</h3>
                    <p>Pull the balloon away from the loop and note how the foil strips move. Do steps 8 and 9 a couple of times so that you can describe the motion of the foil strips well. Record your observations.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 10: Direct Contact Test</h3>
                    <p>Bring the balloon near the loop one more time. This time, however, allow the balloon to touch the paper clip. Record what happens to the foil strips.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 11: Observe After Contact</h3>
                    <p>Pull the balloon away. This time, the behavior of the foil strips should be noticeably different from what it was in step 9. Record the difference.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 12: Test Finger Touch</h3>
                    <p>I want you to do one more thing. These next few steps might be a little tricky. Touch the loop with your finger. You should notice that the foil strips respond to your touch. Record what they do.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 13: Remove Finger</h3>
                    <p>Take your finger away from the loop.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 14: Test Again</h3>
                    <p>Bring the balloon close to the loop, but do not touch it with the balloon. When you see the foil strips move significantly, hold the balloon where it is and touch the paper clip with your finger. From your other hand, as soon as your finger touches the paper clip, the foil strips should move again. Keep your finger resting on the paper clip for a moment.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 15: Final Observation</h3>
                    <p>Pull your finger away so that it is no longer touching the paper clip.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 16: Test Final State</h3>
                    <p>Now pull the balloon away. The foil strips should move yet again and behave similarly to what you saw in step 11. This doesn't always work the first time, so try it a few times until it eventually works. Record your observations.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 17: Van de Graaff Generator Setup (Optional)</h3>
                    <p>If you have access to a Van de Graaff generator, set it up on a non-conductive surface. Make sure it is unplugged before setup. Have an adult supervise this part of the experiment.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 18: Generator Safety</h3>
                    <p>Before turning on the generator, ensure everyone is wearing eye protection. Keep all electronic devices at least 3 feet away from the generator. People with pacemakers or other electronic medical devices should maintain a safe distance.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 19: Charging the Generator</h3>
                    <p>Have an adult turn on the Van de Graaff generator. Allow it to run for about 30 seconds to build up a charge on the metal dome.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 20: Testing with Electroscope</h3>
                    <p>Carefully bring your electroscope near the Van de Graaff generator (about 1-2 feet away). Observe what happens to the foil strips. Move the electroscope closer and further away from the generator and note how the foil strips respond. Do not touch the generator with the electroscope.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 21: Testing with Metal Objects</h3>
                    <p>Hold a small metal object (like a paper clip) using an insulated handle or plastic ruler. Bring it near the Van de Graaff generator dome without touching it. Then touch the metal object to the electroscope's paper clip loop. Observe what happens to the foil strips.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 22: Comparing Charge Strength</h3>
                    <p>Compare the response of the electroscope when charged by the balloon versus when charged indirectly by the Van de Graaff generator. Note any differences in how far the foil strips spread apart and how long they remain charged.</p>
                </div>
                
                <div class="step">
                    <h3>Step 23: Clean Up</h3>
                    <p>Turn off and unplug the Van de Graaff generator. Clean up and put everything away in its proper place.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Data Collection Section -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Data Collection</h2>
            <div class="data-table">
                <h3>Electroscope Observations</h3>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Step</th>
                            <th>Action</th>
                            <th>Foil Strip Behavior</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>7-8</td>
                            <td>Charged balloon near loop (no contact)</td>
                            <td><textarea class="observation-input" placeholder="Describe how the foil strips moved..."></textarea></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>10</td>
                            <td>Balloon touches paper clip</td>
                            <td><textarea class="observation-input" placeholder="Describe what happened to the foil strips..."></textarea></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>11</td>
                            <td>Balloon pulled away after contact</td>
                            <td><textarea class="observation-input" placeholder="How did the behavior change?"></textarea></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>12</td>
                            <td>Finger touches loop</td>
                            <td><textarea class="observation-input" placeholder="What did the foil strips do?"></textarea></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>14-16</td>
                            <td>Induction charging process</td>
                            <td><textarea class="observation-input" placeholder="Describe the complete sequence of movements..."></textarea></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>20</td>
                            <td>Electroscope near Van de Graaff generator</td>
                            <td><textarea class="observation-input" placeholder="Describe how the foil strips responded..."></textarea></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>21</td>
                            <td>Metal object transfer from generator to electroscope</td>
                            <td><textarea class="observation-input" placeholder="Describe what happened to the foil strips..."></textarea></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>22</td>
                            <td>Comparing balloon vs. Van de Graaff charging</td>
                            <td><textarea class="observation-input" placeholder="Note differences in strength and duration..."></textarea></td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Analysis Questions -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Analysis Questions</h2>
            <div class="questions">
                <div class="question">
                    <label for="question1">1. Explain what happened in steps 7-8 when you brought the charged balloon near the electroscope without touching it. What type of charging occurred?</label>
                    <textarea id="question1" rows="3" placeholder="Enter your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                
                <div class="question">
                    <label for="question2">2. What happened when the balloon actually touched the paper clip in step 10? How did this demonstrate charging by conduction?</label>
                    <textarea id="question2" rows="3" placeholder="Enter your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                
                <div class="question">
                    <label for="question3">3. Explain the difference between what you observed in step 9 versus step 11. Why did the foil strips behave differently after the balloon touched the electroscope?</label>
                    <textarea id="question3" rows="4" placeholder="Enter your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                
                <div class="question">
                    <label for="question4">4. Describe the charging by induction process that occurred in steps 14-16. How were you able to charge the electroscope without the balloon ever touching it?</label>
                    <textarea id="question4" rows="4" placeholder="Enter your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                
                <div class="question">
                    <label for="question5">5. Compare and contrast the three methods of charging objects: friction, conduction, and induction. Give an example of each from your experiment.</label>
                    <textarea id="question5" rows="5" placeholder="Enter your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                
                <div class="question">
                    <label for="question6">6. How did the electroscope respond differently when charged using the Van de Graaff generator compared to the balloon? Explain why there was a difference in the strength of the charge.</label>
                    <textarea id="question6" rows="4" placeholder="Enter your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
                
                <div class="question">
                    <label for="question7">7. Explain how the Van de Graaff generator produces a charge. How is this similar to or different from charging by friction?</label>
                    <textarea id="question7" rows="4" placeholder="Enter your answer..."></textarea>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Conclusions Section -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Conclusions</h2>
            <label for="conclusions">Write a paragraph describing what you learned about electrical charge detection and the three methods of charging objects. Explain how the electroscope works and what each charging method demonstrated:</label>
            <textarea id="conclusions" rows="6" placeholder="Write your conclusions here..."></textarea>
        </div>

        <!-- Extension Activities -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Extension Activities</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Try using different materials to charge the balloon (wool, silk, plastic)</li>
                <li>Test what happens with different objects near the electroscope (plastic comb, glass rod)</li>
                <li>Research how professional electroscopes are used in scientific laboratories</li>
                <li>Investigate how electroscopes were historically important in early electrical research</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Submit Button -->
        <div class="submit-section">
            <button id="submit-experiment" class="submit-button">Submit Experiment Results</button>
            <div id="submission-status" class="submission-status"></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Load EmailJS -->
    <script type="text/javascript" src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@emailjs/browser@3/dist/email.min.js" is:inline></script>
    <script type="text/javascript" is:inline>
        // Initialize EmailJS
        (function() {
            emailjs.init("ct_s19oZHppLy9BlW");
        })();

        // Set today's date
        document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
            const today = new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0];
            document.getElementById('experiment-date').value = today;
            
            // Load saved email if available
            const savedEmail = localStorage.getItem('studentEmail');
            if (savedEmail) {
                document.getElementById('student-email').value = savedEmail;
            }
        });

        // Submit experiment results
        document.getElementById('submit-experiment').addEventListener('click', function() {
            const studentName = document.getElementById('student-name').value;
            const studentEmail = document.getElementById('student-email').value;
            const experimentDate = document.getElementById('experiment-date').value;
            
            // Save email for future use
            if (studentEmail) {
                localStorage.setItem('studentEmail', studentEmail);
            }
            
            // Collect all data
            const observations = document.querySelectorAll('.observation-input');
            const answers = [
                document.getElementById('question1').value,
                document.getElementById('question2').value,
                document.getElementById('question3').value,
                document.getElementById('question4').value,
                document.getElementById('question5').value,
                document.getElementById('question6').value,
                document.getElementById('question7').value
            ];
            const conclusions = document.getElementById('conclusions').value;
            
            // Validate required fields
            if (!studentName || !studentEmail) {
                alert('Please fill in your name and email before submitting.');
                return;
            }
            
            // Prepare email content
            const emailContent = `
Student: ${studentName}
Email: ${studentEmail}
Date: ${experimentDate}

Electroscope Observations:
1. Charged balloon near loop (no contact): ${observations[0].value}
2. Balloon touches paper clip: ${observations[1].value}
3. Balloon pulled away after contact: ${observations[2].value}
4. Finger touches loop: ${observations[3].value}
5. Induction charging process: ${observations[4].value}
6. Electroscope near Van de Graaff generator: ${observations[5].value}
7. Metal object transfer from generator to electroscope: ${observations[6].value}
8. Comparing balloon vs. Van de Graaff charging: ${observations[7].value}

Analysis Questions:
1. ${answers[0]}
2. ${answers[1]}
3. ${answers[2]}
4. ${answers[3]}
5. ${answers[4]}
6. ${answers[5]}
7. ${answers[6]}

Conclusions:
${conclusions}
            `;
            
            // Show loading status
            const statusDiv = document.getElementById('submission-status');
            statusDiv.innerHTML = '<p style="color: #3498db;">Submitting experiment results...</p>';
            
            // Send email using EmailJS
            // Prepare comprehensive experiment data for email
            const experimentData = `
EXPERIMENT: Making and Using an Electroscope

STUDENT: ${studentName}
EMAIL: ${studentEmail}

${emailContent}
            `.trim();

            const templateParams = {
                student_name: studentName,
                student_email: studentEmail,
                experiment_title: 'Experiment 11.2: Making and Using an Electroscope',
                hypothesis: 'Exploring electrostatic charge detection using an electroscope',
                missing_elements: 'Complete electroscope construction and testing observations',
                conclusion: experimentData,
                subject: `Physical Science Experiment 11.2 Results - ${studentName}`
            };
            
            emailjs.send('service_ot1jg6s', 'template_y556rhr', templateParams)
                .then(function(response) {
                    console.log('SUCCESS!', response.status, response.text);
                    statusDiv.innerHTML = '<p style="color: #27ae60;">✓ Experiment results submitted successfully!</p>';
                }, function(error) {
                    console.log('FAILED...', error);
                    statusDiv.innerHTML = '<p style="color: #e74c3c;">✗ Failed to submit results. Please try again.</p>';
                });
        });
    </script>

    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            line-height: 1.6;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 20px;
            background-color: #f5f5f5;
        }

        .container {
            max-width: 1000px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            background: white;
            padding: 30px;
            border-radius: 10px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
        }

        h1 {
            color: #2c3e50;
            text-align: center;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
            border-bottom: 3px solid #3498db;
            padding-bottom: 10px;
        }

        .experiment-description {
            text-align: center;
            color: #7f8c8d;
            margin-bottom: 30px;
            font-style: italic;
            font-size: 18px;
        }

        .student-info {
            background: #ecf0f1;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            margin-bottom: 30px;
        }

        .student-info h2 {
            margin-top: 0;
            color: #2c3e50;
        }

        .info-grid {
            display: grid;
            grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));
            gap: 15px;
        }

        .info-grid label {
            display: block;
            font-weight: bold;
            margin-bottom: 5px;
            color: #2c3e50;
        }

        .info-grid input {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 8px;
            border: 1px solid #bdc3c7;
            border-radius: 4px;
            box-sizing: border-box;
        }

        .section {
            margin-bottom: 30px;
        }

        .section h2 {
            color: #2c3e50;
            border-bottom: 2px solid #3498db;
            padding-bottom: 5px;
        }

        .materials-list {
            background: #f8f9fa;
            padding: 15px;
            border-left: 4px solid #3498db;
            margin: 15px 0;
        }

        .materials-list li {
            margin-bottom: 8px;
        }

        .safety-section {
            background: #fff3cd;
            border: 1px solid #ffeaa7;
            border-radius: 8px;
            padding: 20px;
            margin: 20px 0;
        }

        .safety-section h2 {
            color: #856404;
            margin-top: 0;
        }

        .safety-section ul {
            margin-bottom: 0;
        }

        .safety-section li {
            margin-bottom: 8px;
            color: #856404;
        }

        .background-info {
            background: #f8f9fa;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            border-left: 4px solid #17a2b8;
        }

        .background-info h3 {
            color: #2c3e50;
            margin-top: 0;
        }

        .procedure-steps {
            counter-reset: step-counter;
        }

        .step {
            background: #f8f9fa;
            margin: 15px 0;
            padding: 15px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            border-left: 4px solid #28a745;
            position: relative;
        }

        .step h3 {
            color: #28a745;
            margin-top: 0;
        }

        .data-table {
            overflow-x: auto;
        }

        .data-table table {
            width: 100%;
            border-collapse: collapse;
            margin: 15px 0;
        }

        .data-table th,
        .data-table td {
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            padding: 12px;
            text-align: left;
        }

        .data-table th {
            background-color: #3498db;
            color: white;
            font-weight: bold;
        }

        .observation-input {
            width: 100%;
            min-height: 60px;
            padding: 8px;
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            border-radius: 4px;
            resize: vertical;
            box-sizing: border-box;
        }

        .questions {
            space-y: 20px;
        }

        .question {
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }

        .question label {
            display: block;
            font-weight: bold;
            margin-bottom: 8px;
            color: #2c3e50;
        }

        .question textarea {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 10px;
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            border-radius: 4px;
            resize: vertical;
            box-sizing: border-box;
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
        }

        textarea {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 10px;
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            border-radius: 4px;
            resize: vertical;
            box-sizing: border-box;
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
        }

        .submit-section {
            text-align: center;
            margin-top: 30px;
            padding-top: 20px;
            border-top: 2px solid #ecf0f1;
        }

        .submit-button {
            background: #27ae60;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            padding: 15px 30px;
